{
  "configuration_id": "config_036",
  "reason": "Evidence: Our best config (config_004, acc=0.635) uses pca_n2000 + imagenet_vit_l + avgpool + weighted fusion, and we've confirmed multimodal > rna_only (~4.5% gain) and weighted > concat (~0.65% acc, ~1.9% F1 gain). However, we have only tested one image encoder (imagenet_vit_l) and one RNA encoder (pca_n2000), leaving major uncertainty about whether the specific image backbone is a critical design choice. Question: config_036 is a clean single-variable swap of the image encoder (imagenet_vit_l → resnet50) while holding all other settings identical to config_004 (pca_n2000, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile). This isolates the effect of image encoder architecture. Why best next: Under a tight budget, establishing whether the image encoder is a bottleneck (or not) is foundational—if resnet50 performs comparably, we know the image side is not the limiting factor and should pivot to RNA encoder or aggregation; if imagenet_vit_l is clearly superior, we confirm our choice and can confidently explore other dimensions. This is more informative than jumping to a multi-variable change (e.g., new RNA encoder + new aggregation) and directly extends our strongest evidence."
}

